  "_grid_note": "READ FROM THE HEIGHT EXPORT BY THE PRODUCER, which is why the origin, tile_px, naming, z-polarity and per-level tile counts are identical to `assets/terrain/tiles/PIN`'s. A layer tile half a pixel off the height tile beneath it is a misalignment nothing reports, because both look like data.",
  "encodings": {
    "u16": {
      "used_by": [
        "slope",
        "distance_to_channel",
        "hand"
      ],
      "dtype": "uint8 x 3",
      "channels": {
        "R": "high byte",
        "G": "low byte",
        "B": "validity: 255 = data, 0 = no data"
      },
      "value": "min + (R * 256 + G) * (max - min) / 65535, where B > 0",
      "_per_layer": "min/max are per layer, under layers.<name>.stats"
    },
    "dir8": {
      "used_by": [
        "aspect"
      ],
      "dtype": "uint8 x 3",
      "channels": {
        "R": "sin(azimuth), round(v * 127 + 128)",
        "G": "cos(azimuth), round(v * 127 + 128)",
        "B": "0 = no data, 128 = flat/aspect undefined, 255 = aspect valid"
      },
      "value": "azimuth_rad = atan2((R - 128) / 127, (G - 128) / 127), clockwise from north, only where B == 255",
      "_why_not_an_angle": "two reasons. (a) An angle is CIRCULAR: 359.9 and 0.1 degrees are neighbours on the ground and opposite ends of a linear code, so any consumer that interpolates reads due south across north. (b) The source declares no nodata and fills with 0.0, which is a legal aspect -- 52.19% of its grid, 99.04% of that outside slope's mask -- so an angle channel cannot say `no aspect here` without spending a direction on it. A component pair has no seam and leaves B free.",
      "worst_angular_error_deg": 0.2941,
      "_error_measured_over": "18,000 angles at 0.02 deg spacing",
      "cardinals_exact": true,
      "_why_this_scale": "`(v + 1) * 127.5` puts zero at an unrepresentable code, so due north decoded 0.2247 deg off -- and north is what this source's undeclared nodata fill produces, so it is the direction most likely to be met and misread. Centring on 128 with a scale of 127 makes all four cardinals exact and measures very slightly better overall. ~~0.64~~ was an estimate written before the measurement; it is 0.2941.",
      "_coarse_levels": "levels above z=0 average the COMPONENTS, not the angle. Averaging angles across a 2x2 straddling north is the same circular error one aggregation up (convention 7)."
    }
  },
  "_encoding_note": "CARRIED HERE BECAUSE THE CLIENT NEEDS IT AND NOTHING COMMITTED HAS IT. Two kinds, and `aspect` is the one to read: it ships as sin/cos with a THREE-state validity byte and never as an angle. Check B before the components -- where B is 0 or 128 the components are zeroed, and zeroed components decode to 225 degrees, a perfectly plausible south-west. That is the one reading that would look right and be meaningless.",
  "cross_repo": {
    "_what": "how each file above could be checked against `source_commit` in a simulation checkout. For this artefact the answer is that it cannot be, and that is DECLARED rather than left as an empty block a checker would read as a missing vendor run.",
